# Ultimate Workflow

This rule is an index, deliberately thin. Always-loaded text is paid for on every single turn whether it is relevant or not, so only what has **no retrieval cue** belongs here. Everything with a cue lives where the cue can fetch it.

## Principles

- **Think before coding.** State assumptions. Ask when unclear. Name tradeoffs rather than picking silently. Truth over agreement -- do not reflexively affirm a framing you can see a gap in.
- **Simplicity first.** The minimum that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.
- **Surgical changes.** Every changed line traces directly to the request.
- **Goal-driven execution.** Define what "done" looks like in verifiable terms, then loop until it is. **Never fake success** -- no silent fallback, swallowed error, or skipped assertion to make something look finished. If it cannot be done, stop and say so.

Full text, with the reasoning behind each: `skills/the-ultimate-workflow-guidelines/SKILL.md`.

## Workflow

Understand → **plan** (confirm with the user) → **test plan** (confirm) → **implement** → blocker protocol → **harvest** → **hand off**.

Both confirmation points are real stops, not politeness. The plan lives on disk as `PLAN-<feature>.md`, which survives context compaction in a way chat history does not.

When a session ends with work still in flight, run `/handoff` (`commands/handoff.md`, shipped with this plugin). It writes `HANDOFF-<topic>.md` so the next agent inherits the in-flight state that no committed document carries. Where it is not installed, write the same document by hand.

Read `skills/the-ultimate-workflow-guidelines/SKILL.md` for the full loop before starting any non-trivial task. Document shapes live in `skills/the-ultimate-workflow-guidelines/references/` -- do not re-invent them each session.

### Running the phases here

Cursor has subagents but no scriptable orchestrator, so the phases are invoked rather than executed. Each has an agent definition under `agents/`:

| Phase | Invoke | Can write |
|---|---|---|
| Explore for the plan | `/uw-explorer`, once per area, in parallel | No |
| Attack the test plan | `/uw-critic`, once per angle | No |
| Implement | `/uw-implementer` | **Yes** |
| Review the diff | `/uw-reviewer`, once per lens | No |
| Harvest lessons | `/uw-harvester` | No |

**What this loses, stated plainly:** the ordering is advisory here, not structural. There is no round counter and no gate — the caps and stops below are numbers you honour, not numbers enforced. On Claude Code the same phases run as scripts and the ordering is guaranteed by construction.

**What it keeps:** a fresh context per phase, one narrow job per agent, and write access confined to the implementer.

Hold the sequence yourself: wait for every parallel agent before synthesising, stop at both confirmation points, and stop after three failed verification rounds rather than trying a fourth.

## When to skip

Typos and formatting. Single-line fixes with an obvious cause. Pure-doc edits. Trivial renames with no behavioural change. When in doubt, err toward the workflow.

